===Suffix===
{{head|de|suffixes|cat2=noun-forming suffixes|plural|-lein|or|-erlein|or|-leins|cat3=diminutive suffixes|g=n}}

# {{non-gloss definition|suffix used to create a [[diminutive]] form; e.g. ''Kind'' → ''Kind'''lein'''''}}

====Usage notes====
* In contemporary Standard German, {{m|de||-lein}} is usual with words whose stems end in ''-ch'' or ''-g''; thus more often {{m|de|Bächlein|t=little brook}} than {{m|de|Bächelchen}}. There is a limited number of other forms that remain in common use, but for the most part {{m|de||-lein}} now has a poetic and archaic tone. The general diminutive suffix in the written language and in northern speech is {{m|de|-chen}}, while southern informal use prefers regional suffixes like {{m|de|-le}}, {{m|de|-li}} (Swabian/Alemannic) and {{m|de|-erl}} (Austro-Bavarian).
* Diminutives in {{m|de||-lein}} generally have umlaut. The endings {{m|de||-e, -en, -el}} are all deleted before the suffix.
* The plural remains unchanged. However, a few nouns with a plural in {{m|de|-er}} may carry the same ending over to the plural diminutive, and a few diminutives referring to people allow a plural in {{m|de||-s}}. This is common with {{m|de|Kindlein}} → {{m|de||Kinderlein}} and {{m|de|Fräulein}} → {{m|de||Fräuleins}}. Most other such forms are rare.

